A||||6||Black88GT||Great, what did I screw up.  (Need help)||||9||
Z||000000||Black88GT||07-15-2001||06:26 PM||||I was wondering why my car wasn't running too well, I discovered the MSD wiring harness was disconnected (had alternator replaced at a shop recently, guess they did it)!  So I went to reconnect it.  Well I thought I remembered how.  But I couldnt get it to start.  So I tried something else, whatever I tried caused smoke from a wire on the harness and smoke from the wiring over near the battery.  So I went in the house to get the instructions (what I should have done in the first place) and figured it out, but it wouldn't start.  I noticed a wire in that harness near the battery was burnt up!  Great.  I took off the harness and noticed there was still wire left.  The wire that was burnt has a black 20 amp(watt?) fuse I think on it, so only the wire after the "fuse" was burnt.  Can I just cut and splice this wire to the remaining wire in the harness?  Did I screw up all the wiring?  Geesh, I don't know how I am going to get to work tomorrow.<P>thx<P>------------------<BR>Jeff (AOL IM - FieroTT)<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif">      <BR><I>"Once you go black, you never go back..."</I><BR><I>"The Customer Can Have Any Color He Wants So Long As It's Black" </I> -Henry Ford<p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000001||Mach10||07-15-2001||06:31 PM||||Well, from an electronics standpoint, splicing in a new wire shouldn't affect anything. this is of course assuming that you haven't fried the rest of the circuitry||||reg||7
Z||000002||rogergarrison||07-15-2001||07:10 PM||||I dont know if Fiero has it. but lots of cars have a 'fuseable link' in the battery to fusebox wiring. It just looks like regular heavy wire but has a bulge in it. it acts like a slow blowing fuse. If it has one, a new one can just be spliced back in too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||07-15-2001||07:15 PM||||roger, thats what this one looks like.  It has a bulge in it and "20" is written on it.  I am hoping I can splice in a new one and be done with it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||07-15-2001||07:31 PM||||It is a fuse link. You need to get it from the dealer. Don't buy the ones at auto parts stores. They only go up to 12 amps anyways.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||07-15-2001||07:48 PM||||great. if its only burnt up on one side of the fuse, it sounds like it did its job before any real damage was done. good luck||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black88GT||07-15-2001||07:59 PM||||Yeah the one side of the wire is perfect and the other side crumbled to bits when I touched it.  Looks like I got away lucky this time.  You can bet I will be much more careful in the future.<P>||||reg||3
